<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikolai Bukharin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikolai Bukharin was a Bolshevik revolutionary and Marxist theorist, editor of Pravda and a leading member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> in the 1920s. He defended Lenin's New Economic Policy and gradual development of agriculture, allied with Stalin against Trotsky and then opposed forced collectivisation, for which he was removed in 1929. Accused of treason in the last great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> show trial, he was shot in March 1938.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikolai-bukharin.jpg" alt="Nikolai Bukharin" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N.I._Bukharin_(1888-1938)_Crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nikolai Bukharin was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 27 September (9 October, new style) 1888, the second son of two schoolteachers. His political life began at sixteen, when he took part in student activities at Moscow University during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Russian Revolution of 1905</a>. He joined the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party in 1906 and became a Bolshevik. In 1910 he was arrested and exiled internally to Onega, but he escaped abroad the next year and met Lenin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a>. He returned to Moscow by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in May 1917, and on 10 October 1917 he was elected to the Central Committee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the October Revolution Bukharin became editor of Pravda. He led the Left Communist faction in 1918 and wrote The ABC of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">Communism</a> with Yevgeni Preobrazhensky. Initially a supporter of war communism, he backed the New Economic Policy in 1921 and became its chief theorist. After Lenin's death in 1924 he became a full member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> against Trotsky, Grigory Zinoviev and Lev Kamenev.</p><p>It was Bukharin who formulated the thesis of 'socialism in one country' that Stalin put forward in 1924. His slogan to the peasants, 'Enrich yourselves!', and his proposal to reach socialism 'at snail's pace' left him open to attack.</p><p>When Stalin turned to rapid industrialisation and agricultural collectivisation, Bukharin was labelled the leader of the Right Opposition. He lost his place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a> and the editorship of Pravda in April 1929 and was expelled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> on 17 November of that year. Reelected to the Central Committee in 1934, he edited Izvestia and was a principal architect of the 1936 Soviet Constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bukharin was arrested on 27 February 1937 and tried in the Trial of the Twenty One, from 2 to 13 March 1938, with Alexei Rykov, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genrikh-yagoda">Genrikh Yagoda</a> and others. The defendants were accused of belonging to a Bloc of Rightists and Trotskyites and of plotting to assassinate Lenin and Stalin, poison <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxim-gorky">Maxim Gorky</a> and partition the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. The French author Romain Rolland wrote to Stalin asking for mercy. Bukharin was executed on 15 March 1938 at the Kommunarka shooting ground. His wife, Anna Larina, was sent to a labour camp but lived to see him officially rehabilitated in 1988 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a>.</p><p>Lenin had called him a most valuable and major theorist of the party, though he doubted that Bukharin's views were fully Marxist. His works include The Economics of the Transition Period (1920) and, written in prison, Philosophical Arabesques.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nikolai Bukharin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Kommunarka shooting ground, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nikolai Bukharin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
